{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AXPcrYDkPnXJj7qhUSmQLe","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Paper Framing — recovery_aware_struct_prune\n2\t\n3\t## Target Venue\n4\t- Primary: EMNLP 2026 Main (ARR May 25 submission)\n5\t- Fallback: EMNLP 2026 Findings\n6\t- Deadline: ARR May 25, 2026\n7\t\n8\t## Core Insight (Redundancy Paradox)\n9\tTraditional structured pruning selects structures to remove based on importance metrics alone (weight magnitude, gradient sensitivity). We show this misses a critical signal for RLVR recovery: **functional uniqueness**. Structures with high functional overlap (high CKA) can be safely pruned because RLVR redistributes their function across retained structures. Structures with low overlap are functionally irreplaceable — once removed, even RLVR cannot recreate their unique contribution. This creates a **Redundancy Paradox**: protecting functionally unique structures matters more for recovery than protecting high-importance structures.\n10\t\n11\tRASP exploits this via S(h) = I(h) × (1-F(h))^α: high overlap → low score → **pruned first**; low overlap → high score → **protected**. The result: RASP preferentially removes functionally redundant structures (whose function RLVR can recover) while Standard pruning may accidentally remove functionally unique structures that happen to have low importance scores. At 20% sparsity, this effect manifests entirely within MLP channel groups (0/112 attention GQA groups are pruned under either method; all 200 pruned structures are MLP groups), consistent with the \"RL Squeezes, SFT Expands\" mechanism (ICLR 2026).\n12\t\n13\t## Core Claims (3)\n14\t\n15\t1. **CKA-aware pruning preserves reasoning ability**: At matched sparsity and parameter count, RASP preserves significantly more post-prune reasoning accuracy than importance-only pruning (MVP evidence: 29% vs 1% GSM8K at 20% sparsity, p<0.0001), and this gap enables qualitatively different recovery outcomes (RASP-pruned models can be recovered via RLVR; standard-pruned models cannot).\n16\t\n17\t2. **Functional overlap predicts per-structure recoverability** *(scope: MLP channel groups at moderate sparsity)*: Per-structure CKA functional overlap F(h) is a stronger predictor of post-recovery accuracy retention than standard importance metrics alone, within the MLP channel groups that are actually pruned at 20-25% sparsity. At these sparsity levels, attention GQA groups consistently rank above all MLP groups in the global importance list (0/112 pruned); the CKA signal's practical effect is selecting *which* MLP groups to prune (N=784, ~200 pruned). Validated via mixed-effects regression (layer as random effect) on MLP groups, controlling for weight norm, gradient importance, and activation magnitude. Cross-component (attention + MLP) validation requires higher sparsity (~40%+).\n18\t\n19\t3. **Recovery method conditions optimal pruning order**: The relative ranking of pruning strategies changes depending on recovery method (RLVR vs SFT). Structures expendable under RLVR recovery may be critical under SFT recovery, consistent with the \"RL Squeezes, SFT Expands\" mechanism — RLVR compresses incorrect paths (tolerates removal of overlapping structures) while SFT expands correct paths (needs preserved trajectory-encoding structures).\n20\t\n21\t## Story Spine\n22\t\n23\t- **Problem**: Structured pruning for reasoning LLMs optimizes for immediate post-pruning accuracy, treating subsequent recovery as independent. At moderate sparsity (20-35%), this disconnect can be catastrophic: standard importance-based pruning collapses reasoning ability to near-zero, making recovery impossible regardless of method. The choice of what to prune determines whether recovery can succeed at all.\n24\t\n25\t- **Existing approaches & gaps**: Self-Healing (2025) shows RLVR recovers pruned reasoning models to 95%+ at 25% compression but uses standard pruning and tests only one ratio. GRASPrune (ACL 2026) provides SOTA budgeted structural pruning but doesn't study reasoning-specific recovery. RESP (Dec 2025) uses reasoning-trace calibration but has no recovery phase. No work studies how the pruning structure choice interacts with RLVR recovery quality, or whether optimal pruning differs for RLVR vs SFT recovery.\n26\t\n27\t- **Our framing**: We introduce Recovery-Aware Structured Pruning (RASP), reframing structural redundancy from a flaw to eliminate into a signal of RLVR recoverability. RASP scores structures by S(h) = I(h) × (1 - F(h))^α, protecting functionally unique reasoning scaffolding while allowing removal of structures whose function can be redistributed through RLVR. This connects to the Lottery Ticket Hypothesis at a new level: LTH asks \"which subnetwork trains well\" (binary); RASP asks \"which structures to remove so the remainder recovers well under a specific training paradigm\" (paradigm-conditioned trainability).\n28\t\n29\t- **Headline result template**: \"At 20% structured sparsity on DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B, RASP preserves 29% GSM8K accuracy (vs 1% for standard pruning), enabling RLVR recovery to X% while standard-pruned models remain unrecoverable. Per-structure CKA overlap explains Y% of variance in recovery outcomes (mixed-effects β = Z, p < 0.001).\"\n30\t\n31\t## Venue Fit\n32\t- EMNLP Efficient Methods track: natural fit for structured pruning + reasoning model recovery\n33\t- Reasoning model compression is timely (DeepSeek-R1, QwQ, etc. driving demand)\n34\t- Empirical rigor + mechanistic analysis aligns with EMNLP's preference for thorough experimentation\n35\t\n36\t## Venue Fit Risks\n37\t- **\"Just another pruning metric\"**: Mitigate with Redundancy Paradox framing, LTH generalization (paradigm-conditioned trainability), and mechanistic analysis showing WHY it works\n38\t- **\"Single model\"**: DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B is the most widely-used open reasoning model; multi-sparsity + multi-method comparison compensates\n39\t- **\"MPruner already uses CKA\"**: Different granularity (intra-layer per-head vs inter-layer), different objective (recoverability prediction vs redundancy removal), different domain (reasoning LLM vs CNN/general transformer)\n40\t- **\"Cross-component pruning not observed\"**: At 20-25% sparsity, only MLP groups are pruned (0 attention groups). Framework scores both types jointly, but global ranking at moderate sparsity naturally prioritizes MLP removal. Mitigate by: (1) honest description of actual pruning distribution, (2) noting the CKA signal still drives 29% vs 1% gap via MLP group reranking, (3) optionally test higher sparsity where attention groups enter pruning range\n41\t\n42\t## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns & Experimental Responses\n43\t\n44\t| Concern | Response | Supporting Experiment |\n45\t|---------|----------|----------------------|\n46\t| Incremental over importance-only pruning | 28pp post-prune gap is qualitative, not incremental; enables vs prevents recovery | exp-001, exp-002 |\n47\t| CKA overlap might correlate with simpler metrics | Scoring ablation: CKA vs magnitude/gradient/random | exp-007 |\n48\t| Single model generalization | Multi-sparsity (20%, 25%, 30%) + multi-method (Standard, GRASPrune, RASP) | exp-003, exp-005 |\n49\t| RLVR vs SFT claim needs controlled comparison | Same pruned models, same data budget, different recovery method | exp-006 |\n50\t| Statistical rigor of correlation claim | Mixed-effects regression with covariates on MLP groups (N=784, ~200 pruned) | exp-008 |\n51\t| Only MLP groups pruned at 20% sparsity | Honest description + CKA still drives 29% vs 1% via MLP reranking + optional higher sparsity test | experiments.tex |\n52\t| Recovery might just reflect post-prune accuracy | Include GRASPrune (different post-prune accuracy profile) to break correlation | exp-005 |\n53\t\n54\t## Key Differentiations\n55\t- **vs MPruner (2024)**: inter-layer CKA for depth pruning in CNNs → RASP uses intra-layer CKA between individual heads for width pruning in reasoning LLMs, optimizing for post-recovery (not immediate) performance\n56\t- **vs Self-Healing (2025)**: tests RLVR recovery with standard pruning → RASP optimizes the pruning decision FOR recovery\n57\t- **vs RESP (Dec 2025)**: reasoning-trace calibration without recovery → RASP includes recovery-awareness in the pruning score\n58\t- **vs GRASPrune (ACL 2026)**: SOTA budgeted pruning without recovery study → GRASPrune is our primary baseline\n59\t- **vs PASER (2025)**: optimizes recovery DATA → RASP optimizes pruning STRUCTURE (complementary)\n60\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3676cd4c-b4e1-40d9-b1c2-15aa6f59b21a","uuid":"12be8a68-e39b-4e8c-bf6e-5df0a1715a9f","timestamp":"2026-05-15T14:45:46.867Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_17/projects/recovery_aware_struct_prune/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260515T140651/worst/paper_framing.md","content":"# Paper Framing — recovery_aware_struct_prune\n\n## Target Venue\n- Primary: EMNLP 2026 Main (ARR May 25 submission)\n- Fallback: EMNLP 2026 Findings\n- Deadline: ARR May 25, 2026\n\n## Core Insight (Redundancy Paradox)\nTraditional structured pruning selects structures to remove based on importance metrics alone (weight magnitude, gradient sensitivity). We show this misses a critical signal for RLVR recovery: **functional uniqueness**. Structures with high functional overlap (high CKA) can be safely pruned because RLVR redistributes their function across retained structures. Structures with low overlap are functionally irreplaceable — once removed, even RLVR cannot recreate their unique contribution. This creates a **Redundancy Paradox**: protecting functionally unique structures matters more for recovery than protecting high-importance structures.\n\nRASP exploits this via S(h) = I(h) × (1-F(h))^α: high overlap → low score → **pruned first**; low overlap → high score → **protected**. The result: RASP preferentially removes functionally redundant structures (whose function RLVR can recover) while Standard pruning may accidentally remove functionally unique structures that happen to have low importance scores. At 20% sparsity, this effect manifests entirely within MLP channel groups (0/112 attention GQA groups are pruned under either method; all 200 pruned structures are MLP groups), consistent with the \"RL Squeezes, SFT Expands\" mechanism (ICLR 2026).\n\n## Core Claims (3)\n\n1. **CKA-aware pruning preserves reasoning ability**: At matched sparsity and parameter count, RASP preserves significantly more post-prune reasoning accuracy than importance-only pruning (MVP evidence: 29% vs 1% GSM8K at 20% sparsity, p<0.0001), and this gap enables qualitatively different recovery outcomes (RASP-pruned models can be recovered via RLVR; standard-pruned models cannot).\n\n2. **Functional overlap predicts per-structure recoverability** *(scope: MLP channel groups at moderate sparsity)*: Per-structure CKA functional overlap F(h) is a stronger predictor of post-recovery accuracy retention than standard importance metrics alone, within the MLP channel groups that are actually pruned at 20-25% sparsity. At these sparsity levels, attention GQA groups consistently rank above all MLP groups in the global importance list (0/112 pruned); the CKA signal's practical effect is selecting *which* MLP groups to prune (N=784, ~200 pruned). Validated via mixed-effects regression (layer as random effect) on MLP groups, controlling for weight norm, gradient importance, and activation magnitude. Cross-component (attention + MLP) validation requires higher sparsity (~40%+).\n\n3. **Recovery method conditions optimal pruning order**: The relative ranking of pruning strategies changes depending on recovery method (RLVR vs SFT). Structures expendable under RLVR recovery may be critical under SFT recovery, consistent with the \"RL Squeezes, SFT Expands\" mechanism — RLVR compresses incorrect paths (tolerates removal of overlapping structures) while SFT expands correct paths (needs preserved trajectory-encoding structures).\n\n## Story Spine\n\n- **Problem**: Structured pruning for reasoning LLMs optimizes for immediate post-pruning accuracy, treating subsequent recovery as independent. At moderate sparsity (20-35%), this disconnect can be catastrophic: standard importance-based pruning collapses reasoning ability to near-zero, making recovery impossible regardless of method. The choice of what to prune determines whether recovery can succeed at all.\n\n- **Existing approaches & gaps**: Self-Healing (2025) shows RLVR recovers pruned reasoning models to 95%+ at 25% compression but uses standard pruning and tests only one ratio. GRASPrune (ACL 2026) provides SOTA budgeted structural pruning but doesn't study reasoning-specific recovery. RESP (Dec 2025) uses reasoning-trace calibration but has no recovery phase. No work studies how the pruning structure choice interacts with RLVR recovery quality, or whether optimal pruning differs for RLVR vs SFT recovery.\n\n- **Our framing**: We introduce Recovery-Aware Structured Pruning (RASP), reframing structural redundancy from a flaw to eliminate into a signal of RLVR recoverability. RASP scores structures by S(h) = I(h) × (1 - F(h))^α, protecting functionally unique reasoning scaffolding while allowing removal of structures whose function can be redistributed through RLVR. This connects to the Lottery Ticket Hypothesis at a new level: LTH asks \"which subnetwork trains well\" (binary); RASP asks \"which structures to remove so the remainder recovers well under a specific training paradigm\" (paradigm-conditioned trainability).\n\n- **Headline result template**: \"At 20% structured sparsity on DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B, RASP preserves 29% GSM8K accuracy (vs 1% for standard pruning), enabling RLVR recovery to X% while standard-pruned models remain unrecoverable. Per-structure CKA overlap explains Y% of variance in recovery outcomes (mixed-effects β = Z, p < 0.001).\"\n\n## Venue Fit\n- EMNLP Efficient Methods track: natural fit for structured pruning + reasoning model recovery\n- Reasoning model compression is timely (DeepSeek-R1, QwQ, etc. driving demand)\n- Empirical rigor + mechanistic analysis aligns with EMNLP's preference for thorough experimentation\n\n## Venue Fit Risks\n- **\"Just another pruning metric\"**: Mitigate with Redundancy Paradox framing, LTH generalization (paradigm-conditioned trainability), and mechanistic analysis showing WHY it works\n- **\"Single model\"**: DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B is the most widely-used open reasoning model; multi-sparsity + multi-method comparison compensates\n- **\"MPruner already uses CKA\"**: Different granularity (intra-layer per-head vs inter-layer), different objective (recoverability prediction vs redundancy removal), different domain (reasoning LLM vs CNN/general transformer)\n- **\"Cross-component pruning not observed\"**: At 20-25% sparsity, only MLP groups are pruned (0 attention groups). Framework scores both types jointly, but global ranking at moderate sparsity naturally prioritizes MLP removal. Mitigate by: (1) honest description of actual pruning distribution, (2) noting the CKA signal still drives 29% vs 1% gap via MLP group reranking, (3) optionally test higher sparsity where attention groups enter pruning range\n\n## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns & Experimental Responses\n\n| Concern | Response | Supporting Experiment |\n|---------|----------|----------------------|\n| Incremental over importance-only pruning | 28pp post-prune gap is qualitative, not incremental; enables vs prevents recovery | exp-001, exp-002 |\n| CKA overlap might correlate with simpler metrics | Scoring ablation: CKA vs magnitude/gradient/random | exp-007 |\n| Single model generalization | Multi-sparsity (20%, 25%, 30%) + multi-method (Standard, GRASPrune, RASP) | exp-003, exp-005 |\n| RLVR vs SFT claim needs controlled comparison | Same pruned models, same data budget, different recovery method | exp-006 |\n| Statistical rigor of correlation claim | Mixed-effects regression with covariates on MLP groups (N=784, ~200 pruned) | exp-008 |\n| Only MLP groups pruned at 20% sparsity | Honest description + CKA still drives 29% vs 1% via MLP reranking + optional higher sparsity test | experiments.tex |\n| Recovery might just reflect post-prune accuracy | Include GRASPrune (different post-prune accuracy profile) to break correlation | exp-005 |\n\n## Key Differentiations\n- **vs MPruner (2024)**: inter-layer CKA for depth pruning in CNNs → RASP uses intra-layer CKA between individual heads for width pruning in reasoning LLMs, optimizing for post-recovery (not immediate) performance\n- **vs Self-Healing (2025)**: tests RLVR recovery with standard pruning → RASP optimizes the pruning decision FOR recovery\n- **vs RESP (Dec 2025)**: reasoning-trace calibration without recovery → RASP includes recovery-awareness in the pruning score\n- **vs GRASPrune (ACL 2026)**: SOTA budgeted pruning without recovery study → GRASPrune is our primary baseline\n- **vs PASER (2025)**: optimizes recovery DATA → RASP optimizes pruning STRUCTURE (complementary)\n","numLines":60,"startLine":1,"totalLines":60}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01W46wGLib3RNQmkHAFYg5Di","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have a thorough understanding of the experiment data and paper framing. Let me write the paper. The key narrative for the \"worst\" tier:\n\n1. Reframe as \"Do Reasoning LLMs Need Recovery-Aware Pruning?\" — a controlled empirical study\n2. Core finding is a surprising negative result: CKA functional overlap does NOT predict RLVR recoverability\n3. BUT α-scaled importance scoring DOES outperform vanilla magnitude pruning (weak connection to CKA theory)\n4. SFT recovery is surprisingly effective for ALL pruning methods\n5. GRPO fails to reverse Standard's advantage established during SFT\n\n\n6. RASP shows no additional gains under GRPO, even slightly worse\n\nThe paper's value comes from its experimental rigor across multiple seeds and sparsity levels, the practical effectiveness of α scoring as a simple pruning improvement, and the empirical evidence challenging conventional recovery-aware pruning assumptions. Now I'm organizing the full LaTeX paper around the key experimental results, starting with the dense baseline performance and working through the pruning and recovery outcomes across all methods.\n\nLooking at the specific numbers: Standard recovery consistently outperforms RASP by meaningful margins—8.2 percentage points with SFT and 4.3 points with GRPO—while GRPO itself provides minimal additional benefit. The α ablation shows a clear pattern where it monotonically improves post-pruning performance but reverses after recovery. Across sparsity levels and out-of-distribution settings, Standard maintains its advantage, with the gap narrowing only at the highest sparsity level where both methods converge.\n\nNow I'm organizing how to structure the paper—starting with the core findings on post-pruning performance and SFT recovery, then moving through GRPO results, the full pipeline comparison, and finally the alternative pruning methods like GRASPrune and Bonsai to show the generality of the approach.\n\nI realize I'm listing too many subsections though. I need to consolidate these results into fewer, more cohesive sections that group related experiments together rather than giving each table its own subsection.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":18889,"cache_read_input_tokens":23139,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":18889},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3676cd4c-b4e1-40d9-b1c2-15aa6f59b21a","uuid":"06806a02-cb87-4508-89f7-bbaf140e1cdb"}
